---
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import "../styles/site.css";

// The privacy policy for Raptor: the Panel, the web app, the phone app,
// and this site. Plain words, and only what's true of the code: when what
// the Panel keeps or who processes it changes, change this page too
// (docs/PANEL.md, docs/DEPLOY.md).
const updated = "November 16, 2026";
const title = "Privacy policy: Raptor";
const description = "What Raptor collects when you use it, why, who else handles it, how long it's kept, and how to have it deleted.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://raptorpanel.net/privacy" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#1a1314" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#f8f5f5" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip button small" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="wrap top">
      <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Raptor, home"><Logo /></a>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="wrap legal">
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="meta">Last updated {updated}</p>

      <p>
        Raptor runs game servers on machines you own, managed from our Panel at app.raptorpanel.net and from
        Raptor's phone app. This page says what we collect to do that, why, who else handles it, and how long
        we keep it. "We" is Xena Studios, which makes Raptor.
      </p>
      <p>
        The short version: we collect what it takes to run your account and your servers, we don't sell it, we
        don't show ads, and we don't track you across other apps or websites.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your account</dt>
        <dd>
          Your email address, the name you give, and your settings. If you sign in with Google, GitHub, or
          Discord, we keep which account it is, and its name and picture.
        </dd>

        <dt>How you sign in</dt>
        <dd>
          Your passkeys' public keys (never the private key, which stays on your device), and your
          authenticator app's secret and recovery codes, encrypted. Email sign-in codes are kept only as
          hashes, and stop working after a few minutes.
        </dd>

        <dt>Your devices and activity</dt>
        <dd>
          For each place you're signed in: the IP address, the browser or device, and when it was last used, so
          you can see and sign out your devices. An activity log of what happens on your account and in your
          teams (sign-ins, changes, server actions), with the IP address and browser of each.
        </dd>

        <dt>Your teams, machines, and servers</dt>
        <dd>
          What you set up in Raptor: your teams and who's in them, your machines (nodes), and your servers:
          their names, settings, state, resource use, and player counts. Your servers' files, worlds, and
          console run on your machines, not ours. When you open a console or move files in the browser, they
          pass through our network to reach you, and aren't stored.
        </dd>

        <dt>Alerts</dt>
        <dd>
          Alerts you've been sent, and the channels you've asked to get them on (email, Telegram, Discord, and
          others). A channel's secrets, such as a webhook address, are encrypted.
        </dd>

        <dt>The phone app</dt>
        <dd>
          Your phone's name and model, and a push token from Apple or Google so we can send it alerts. If you
          turn on sign-in approvals, a secret kept on your phone behind Face ID, and its counterpart on our side.
          The app doesn't use your location, contacts, photos, or advertising identifier.
        </dd>

        <dt>Billing</dt>
        <dd>
          Your plan, what you've paid, and when. Payments are handled by Polar; we never see your card number.
        </dd>

        <dt>Support</dt>
        <dd>
          What you send us when you ask for help. Our staff can reach one of your machines only if you approve
          it, for the time you allow, and everything they do is in your team's activity log.
        </dd>

        <dt>Backups</dt>
        <dd>
          If you use Raptor Backup Storage, your servers' backups are stored for you, encrypted by your machine
          before they're uploaded.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>What we use it for</h2>
      <p>
        To run Raptor: signing you in, running and showing your servers, sending your alerts, billing, keeping
        accounts safe (spotting sign-in abuse and showing you where you're signed in), answering support, and
        keeping the service working (we measure how it performs, not what you do). We email you about your
        account and your servers, not marketing, unless you ask for it.
      </p>

      <h2>Who else handles it</h2>
      <p>We use these companies to run Raptor, and they handle your data only to do that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Oracle Cloud</strong> hosts the Panel and its database, in the United States.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> carries traffic to the Panel and the web app, and checks sign-in forms
          for bots (Turnstile).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Resend</strong> sends our emails.</li>
        <li><strong>Backblaze</strong> stores the Panel's backups, and Raptor Backup Storage.</li>
        <li><strong>Polar</strong> handles payments.</li>
        <li><strong>Grafana Cloud</strong> receives performance measurements from the Panel.</li>
        <li><strong>Apple and Google</strong> deliver the phone app's notifications.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google, GitHub, and Discord</strong>, only if you sign in with them; and Telegram, Discord,
          Slack, ntfy, or a webhook, only if you add one as an alert channel.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>We don't sell your data, or share it with anyone else, unless the law requires us to.</p>

      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your account and what's in it: until you delete it.</li>
        <li>Sign-ins: 30 days unused, and 90 days at most in a browser.</li>
        <li>The activity log: a year.</li>
        <li>Alerts in your inbox, and the history of where they were sent: 30 days.</li>
        <li>The phone app's push token: until you sign out of the app, or remove it from your devices.</li>
        <li>The Panel's backups: four weeks, then they roll over.</li>
        <li>Billing records: as long as the law requires.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can see and change your account, your devices, and your activity in the web app's settings, and
        delete your account there. Deleting it removes your account and its activity; your teams' logs keep a
        line saying you left. To get a copy of your data, or for anything you can't do yourself, email
        <a href="mailto:support@raptorpanel.net">support@raptorpanel.net</a>. Depending on where you live, you
        may have more rights over your data; we'll honour them.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Raptor isn't meant for children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect their data.</p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        When this policy changes, we'll update the date at the top, and tell you by email if it's a change
        that matters.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:support@raptorpanel.net">support@raptorpanel.net</a>. For security problems, see
        our <a href="https://github.com/xena-studios/raptor/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.
      </p>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <div class="wrap">
        <span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Xena Studios</span>
        <nav aria-label="Footer">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <a href="https://docs.raptorpanel.net">Docs</a>
          <a href="https://status.raptorpanel.net">Status</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .legal {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 2 * var(--page));
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
  }
  .legal h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .legal h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .legal p,
  .legal li,
  .legal dd {
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .legal .meta {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .legal dt {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .legal dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .legal ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .legal li + li {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  .legal a {
    color: var(--red);
  }
</style>
